;; priv/stdlib/core.logos
;;
;; `logos.core`'s self-hosted layer, loaded into the `logos.core` namespace
;; at startup. `Logos.Stdlib.load!/1` reads this file and evaluates every
;; top-level form here with the current namespace set to `logos.core`, so
;; every `def`/`defmacro` below interns into `logos.core` -- visible
;; everywhere else via the implicit-core-refer mechanism (see
;; `Logos.Namespace`'s moduledoc). `defmacro` itself is bootstrapped from
;; `fn`/`cond` (real special forms) plus `list`/`cons`/`with-meta`
;; (Layer-1 primitives) below, since every macro after it needs `defmacro`
;; to already exist.
;;
;; This is one of four files under `priv/stdlib/` -- `seq.logos`
;; (`logos.seq`) and `concurrency.logos` (`logos.concurrency`) load into
;; their own namespaces after this one, then `Logos.Stdlib.load!/1` refers
;; both of them (plus the primitives-only `logos.map`) into `logos.core`
;; itself, which is what makes their public functions/macros reachable
;; unqualified from anywhere, exactly like everything defined directly in
;; this file.
;;
;; ## Metadata: `:private`, `:macro`, and `:dynamic`
;;
;; `def`'s symbol position accepts `^`-attached metadata (reader sugar)
;; or a `with-meta`-returned symbol (for a name built as macro-generated
;; data, e.g. `defn-` below); `Logos.Eval.eval_def/5` reads a fixed,
;; known set of flags off it (`Logos.Eval`'s private `meta_from_symbol/2`):
;; `^:private` hides a var from `require`/`use :refer` (though NOT from a
;; direct qualified reference, e.g. `logos.core/build-let` still resolves
;; even though `build-let` is private -- privacy is enforced at `refer!`
;; time, not at qualified-lookup time), `^:macro` is an alternative to
;; a separate `(set-macro! 'name true)` call, both flagging the same
;; `meta[:macro] == true` `Logos.Macroexpand` checks, and `^:dynamic`
;; marks a var as bindable via `binding` (below) -- see that section's
;; own comment.
;;
;; **Important, non-obvious constraint on `^:private` for a HELPER
;; function**: Logos has no notion of "a closure's defining namespace" --
;; every bare-symbol reference (inside a function OR macro body) resolves
;; against `Logos.Runtime.current_ns/1` **at the moment that body actually
;; runs**, which is the CALLER's current namespace, not wherever the
;; function/macro was originally `def`'d. A private helper directly in
;; `logos.core` (like `build-let`/`ns-clause-forms` below) is still safe
;; to call from a public `logos.core` macro's body regardless of caller
;; namespace, because the auto-refer-core fallback's *first* branch
;; (`Namespace.has_var?(runtime, "logos.core", name)`) doesn't check
;; privacy at all -- only `refer!`'s enumeration does. But a helper that
;; instead lives in `logos.seq`/`logos.map`/`logos.concurrency` is only
;; reachable from another namespace via `logos.core`'s own *refer* of
;; that namespace, which DOES filter by `public?` -- marking such a
;; helper `^:private` would silently break the public function/macro that
;; calls it as soon as that public function/macro is invoked from
;; anywhere other than its own defining namespace (i.e., always, in
;; practice). See `seq.logos`/`concurrency.logos` for the concrete cases
;; this rules out (`reverse-onto`, `receive-split` & friends).
;;
;; ## Syntax-quote and hygiene
;;
;; Backtick/unquote/unquote-splice (`` ` ``/`~`/`~@`) desugar into
;; `list`/`concat`/`quote` calls at read time (see
;; `lib/logos/reader/actions.ex`); free symbols inside a syntax-quote
;; auto-qualify against the current namespace, and `x#` auto-gensyms
;; consistently within one syntax-quote expansion (every occurrence of a
;; given `x#` in one expansion gets the same fresh symbol, but a new
;; expansion gets a different one). This is what gives macros hygiene: a
;; macro's own internal temporaries can never collide with a symbol the
;; macro's caller happens to use. Every macro below is built with
;; syntax-quote except `defmacro`'s own bootstrap, which must stay
;; expressible *without* syntax-quote, since `defmacro` itself is what
;; makes syntax-quote-using macros possible to write in the first place;
;; and `build-let`, an ordinary non-macro recursive helper -- syntax-quote
;; only changes how *macros* build their expansion, it has nothing to do
;; with an ordinary recursive function like this one.
;;
;; ## and/or: real Clojure "last value" semantics
;;
;; `and`/`or` evaluate each form at most once and return the
;; last-evaluated value rather than a plain `true`/`false` (`(and 1 2)`
;; => `2`, `(or false 5)` => `5`), while still short-circuiting. This
;; requires binding each non-final form's value to a hygienic temporary
;; (`g#`, auto-gensym'd by syntax-quote) so it can be tested for
;; truthiness and then returned without re-evaluating it -- not safe to
;; hand-roll without auto-gensym, since a hand-written temp name could
;; collide with a caller's own binding of the same name.
;; ---- defmacro bootstrap -------------------------------------------------------

(def ^:macro defmacro
  "Defines a macro: like `defn`, but calls to the resulting var expand at
  macro-expansion time (against the caller's unevaluated argument forms)
  instead of being an ordinary function call. Supports an optional
  docstring, exactly like `defn`: `(defmacro name \"doc\" [params]
  body...)` or `(defmacro name [params] body...)`.

  Bootstrapped from only `fn`/`cond` (real special forms, available
  before any stdlib file loads) plus `list`/`cons`/`first`/`rest`/
  `with-meta`/`string?` (Layer-1 primitives, installed before any stdlib
  file loads too) -- no syntax-quote, no `let`/`if`, since `defmacro`
  itself is what makes those comfortable to write for every macro below.
  `with-meta` is called as an ordinary function argument here (evaluated
  immediately, producing a real `:macro`-flagged symbol *value*) rather
  than spliced in as an unevaluated call form -- `def`'s symbol position
  is never itself evaluated, it must already structurally be a symbol by
  the time `def` sees it. The trailing rest-param is named `more`, not
  `rest` -- naming it `rest` would shadow the `rest` *primitive* this
  same body needs to call on it."
  (fn [name params-or-doc & more]
    ((fn [macro-name]
        (cond
          (string? params-or-doc)
          (list 'def macro-name params-or-doc
            (cons 'fn (cons (first more) (rest more))))
          true
          (list 'def macro-name (cons 'fn (cons params-or-doc more)))))
      (with-meta name {:macro true}))))
;; ---- destructuring (shared by let/defn/defn- below) -----------------------
;; A pattern is a plain symbol (base case -- binds directly), a vector
;; (positional/`&`-rest/`:as`), or a map (`:keys`/explicit `name :key`
;; pairs/`:as`); nested patterns recurse. `destructure` expands ONE
;; (pattern, value-form) pair into a flat list of (plain-symbol,
;; value-form) pairs, ready to splice straight into `build-let`'s own
;; pairs list -- a compound pattern binds its whole value to a fresh
;; `gensym`'d temp FIRST, then extracts every part from that temp (never
;; re-evaluating the original value-form more than once).
;;
;; **Every helper here is written using ONLY Layer-1 primitives plus
;; `cond`/`fn` (never `let`, never a `logos.seq` function like
;; `map`/`reduce`/`reverse`/`empty?`/`nth`)** -- a hard bootstrap
;; constraint, not a style preference. `defn`'s own macro body (below)
;; calls into this machinery on every `defn` it expands, including the
;; very first one in this file (the type-predicates section), which runs
;; long before `logos.seq` loads; `build-let`'s own definition can't use
;; `let` either, since `let` itself isn't defined yet at the point
;; `build-let` is (same reasoning as `defmacro`'s own bootstrap avoiding
;; `let`/syntax-quote -- see this file's header comment). Confirmed the
;; hard way once already this session (see `defn`'s own multi-arity
;; support and its `vector?` bootstrap bug) -- not repeating that mistake
;; here.

(def ^:private destructure
  "Expands one destructuring pattern into a flat list of (name
  value-form) pairs."
  (fn [pattern value]
    (cond
      (= (type-of pattern) :vector) (destructure-vector pattern value)
      (= (type-of pattern) :map) (destructure-map pattern value)
      true (list pattern value))))
(def ^:private destructure-vector
  "`[a b]`/`[a b & more]`/`[a b :as whole]` -- binds a fresh temp to
  `value` first, then walks `pattern`'s elements against it. An
  immediately-invoked `fn` stands in for a single-binding `let` here
  (see this section's header comment for why `let` itself is off
  limits)."
  (fn [pattern value]
    ((fn [g] (concat (list g value) (destructure-vector-positions (to-list pattern) g 0)))
      (gensym "vec"))))
(def ^:private destructure-vector-positions
  (fn [items temp index]
    (cond
      (= items ()) ()
      (= (first items) '&)
      (concat
        (destructure (first (rest items)) (list 'drop index temp))
        (destructure-vector-positions (rest (rest items)) temp index))
      (= (first items) (keyword "as"))
      (concat
        (list (first (rest items)) temp)
        (destructure-vector-positions (rest (rest items)) temp index))
      true
      (concat
        (destructure (first items) (list 'nth temp index))
        (destructure-vector-positions (rest items) temp (+ index 1))))))
(def ^:private destructure-map
  "`{:keys [a b]}`/`{name :key}`/`{:as whole}` -- binds a fresh temp to
  `value` first, then walks `pattern`'s entries against it. Does NOT
  support `:strs`/`:syms`/`:or` (default values) -- see ROADMAP.md."
  (fn [pattern value]
    ((fn [g] (concat (list g value) (destructure-map-entries (to-list pattern) g)))
      (gensym "map"))))
(def ^:private destructure-map-entries
  (fn [entries temp]
    (cond
      (= entries ()) ()
      true
      (destructure-map-entry (first (first entries)) (first (rest (first entries))) temp
        (destructure-map-entries (rest entries) temp)))))
;; Split out from `destructure-map-entries` itself only because this
;; file's own bootstrap constraint (see this section's header comment)
;; rules out `let` -- `k`/`v` would otherwise need naming twice over
;; (once per `cond` branch) without one.
(def ^:private destructure-map-entry
  (fn [k v temp more]
    (cond
      (= k (keyword "keys")) (concat (destructure-map-keys (to-list v) temp) more)
      (= k (keyword "as")) (concat (list v temp) more)
      true (concat (destructure k (list 'get temp v)) more))))
(def ^:private destructure-map-keys
  (fn [names temp]
    (cond
      (= names ()) ()
      true
      (concat
        (list (first names) (list 'get temp (keyword (first names))))
        (destructure-map-keys (rest names) temp)))))
;; ---- let --------------------------------------------------------------------
;; `build-let` is an ordinary (non-macro), private helper: given the
;; bindings vector already flattened to a list (`[a 1 b 2]` -> `(a 1 b
;; 2)`) and the body forms, it builds nested immediately-invoked
;; single-arg `fn`s: `(let [a 1 b 2] body)` -> `((fn [a] ((fn [b] (do
;; body)) 2)) 1)`. `^:private` is safe here -- see this file's header
;; comment for why (`build-let` lives directly in `logos.core`). A
;; vector/map pattern in a binding's name position (instead of a plain
;; symbol) is expanded via `destructure` first, splicing its flat
;; (name value) pairs into the pairs list in place and recursing --
;; `build-let` itself, like the destructuring helpers above, avoids
;; `let` in its own body for the same bootstrap reason.

(def ^:private build-let
  "Non-public helper backing `let`'s expansion -- see this section's
  header comment for the desugaring it performs."
  (fn [pairs body]
    (cond
      (= pairs ()) (cons 'do body)
      (= (type-of (first pairs)) :vector)
      (build-let
        (concat (destructure (first pairs) (first (rest pairs))) (rest (rest pairs)))
        body)
      (= (type-of (first pairs)) :map)
      (build-let
        (concat (destructure (first pairs) (first (rest pairs))) (rest (rest pairs)))
        body)
      true (list
        (cons 'fn (cons (vector (first pairs)) (list (build-let (rest (rest pairs)) body))))
        (first (rest pairs))))))
(defmacro let
  "Introduces local bindings: `(let [a 1 b 2] body...)` evaluates the
  body forms in order with each name bound to its paired value, later
  bindings seeing earlier ones (like Clojure's `let*`, not simultaneous
  binding). A binding's name position accepts a destructuring pattern
  too, not just a plain symbol -- `(let [[a b] pair] ...)` (vector,
  positional/`&`/`:as`) or `(let [{:keys [a b]} m] ...)` (map,
  `:keys`/explicit/`:as`), nested patterns included. `fn`/`defmacro`
  don't support this in their own param positions (only `let`/`defn`/
  `defn-` do, all macros that can expand patterns before handing a
  plain-symbol-only params list to the real `fn` special form) -- see
  ROADMAP.md."
  [bindings & body]
  (build-let (to-list bindings) body))
;; ---- if -----------------------------------------------------------------------

(defmacro if
  "`(if test then else?)` -- evaluates `then` if `test` is truthy
  (anything but `nil`/`false`), else `else` (or `nil` if omitted).
  Implemented as a macro built directly on the `cond` special form."
  [test then & else]
  `(cond ~test ~then true ~(cond (= else ()) nil true (first else))))
;; ---- when / unless --------------------------------------------------------

(defmacro when
  "`(when test body...)` -- runs `body` (an implicit `do`) if `test` is
  truthy, else returns `nil` without evaluating `body` at all."
  [test & body]
  `(cond ~test (do ~@body) true nil))
(defmacro unless
  "`(unless test body...)` -- the inverse of `when`: runs `body` only
  when `test` is falsy."
  [test & body]
  `(cond ~test nil true (do ~@body)))
;; ---- and / or ---------------------------------------------------------------
;; Real Clojure "last value" semantics (see the header comment above):
;; each form is evaluated at most once, via a gensym'd temp so a caller's
;; own use of the same-looking name can never be captured or collide
;; with it.

(defmacro and
  "Evaluates each form in order, short-circuiting to the first falsy
  value; if every form is truthy, returns the LAST form's value (not a
  bare `true`) -- real Clojure semantics, not C-style boolean `and`.
  `(and)` is `true`."
  [& forms]
  (cond
    (= forms ()) true
    (= (rest forms) ()) (first forms)
    true `(let [g# ~(first forms)] (cond g# (and ~@(rest forms)) true g#))))
(defmacro or
  "Evaluates each form in order, short-circuiting to the first truthy
  value (returned verbatim, not coerced to `true`); if every form is
  falsy, returns the last one. `(or)` is `false`."
  [& forms]
  (cond
    (= forms ()) false
    (= (rest forms) ()) (first forms)
    true `(let [g# ~(first forms)] (cond g# g# true (or ~@(rest forms))))))
;; ---- defn / defn- destructuring in param positions -------------------------
;; `fn` itself (the special form) never supports a pattern in a param
;; position -- only `defn`/`defn-` (macros, like `let`) do, expanding
;; each pattern into a fresh gensym'd plain-symbol param plus a `let`
;; wrapping the body to reconstruct it, reusing the exact same
;; `destructure` machinery `let` itself uses above. Safe to use `let`
;; freely in the helpers below (unlike `build-let`/`destructure*`
;; above) -- `let` is already fully defined by this point in the file.

(def ^:private destructure-params-onto
  (fn [items]
    (if (= items ())
      (list () ())
      (let [item (first items)
          rest-result (destructure-params-onto (rest items))
          rest-params (first rest-result)
          rest-bindings (first (rest rest-result))]
        (cond
          (= (type-of item) :vector)
          (let [g (gensym "param")]
            (list (cons g rest-params) (concat (destructure item g) rest-bindings)))
          (= (type-of item) :map)
          (let [g (gensym "param")]
            (list (cons g rest-params) (concat (destructure item g) rest-bindings)))
          true (list (cons item rest-params) rest-bindings))))))
(def ^:private destructure-params
  "Walks a `fn` params vector, replacing each destructuring pattern with
  a fresh gensym and collecting the bindings needed to reconstruct it.
  Returns a 2-element list: the new, plain-symbol-only params vector
  `fn` itself can accept, and a flat bindings list (empty if `params`
  had no patterns at all)."
  (fn [params]
    (let [result (destructure-params-onto (to-list params))]
      (list (list->vector (first result)) (first (rest result))))))
(def ^:private destructure-fn-body
  "Given one arity's params vector and body forms, returns a 2-element
  list: the params `fn` can accept, and the body (wrapped in a `let`
  reconstructing any patterns, unchanged if there were none)."
  (fn [params body]
    (let [result (destructure-params params)
        new-params (first result)
        bindings (first (rest result))]
      (if (= bindings ())
        (list params body)
        (list new-params (list (cons 'let (cons (list->vector bindings) body))))))))
(def ^:private destructure-fn-clause
  "`destructure-fn-body`'s multi-arity counterpart: one `(params
  body...)` clause in, one expanded clause out."
  (fn [clause]
    (let [expanded (destructure-fn-body (first clause) (rest clause))]
      (cons (first expanded) (first (rest expanded))))))
(def ^:private destructure-fn-clauses
  "Maps `destructure-fn-clause` over every arity clause."
  (fn [clauses]
    (cond
      (= clauses ()) ()
      true
      (cons (destructure-fn-clause (first clauses)) (destructure-fn-clauses (rest clauses))))))
;; ---- defn / defn- ---------------------------------------------------------------
;; Both `defn`/`defn-` need to tell apart four shapes of their own
;; trailing arguments: single-arity with/without a leading docstring
;; (`params-or-doc` is a string, or is itself the params vector), and
;; multi-arity with/without one (`params-or-doc` is a string followed by
;; arity-clause lists in `more`, or `params-or-doc` itself already IS the
;; first arity-clause list -- a bare params vector and an arity-clause
;; list are the only two possible shapes once it's not a docstring, so
;; `(= (type-of ...) :vector)` tells them apart). That check is spelled
;; out via the `type-of` *primitive* directly here, deliberately NOT the
;; `vector?` wrapper defined later in this same file -- `defn`'s own
;; macro body runs the moment ANY `defn` call is expanded, including the
;; very first one (`(defn nil? ...)`, in the type-predicates section
;; below), which is *itself* defined via `defn` and so necessarily runs
;; before `vector?` exists yet. Confirmed the hard way: an earlier draft
;; that called `vector?` here broke `Logos.Stdlib.load!/1` itself with
;; `{:unbound_symbol, "vector?"}`, since `vector?` isn't defined until
;; several `defn` calls after this file's very first one. The multi-arity
;; `fn` value itself has to be built as plain data (`cons`, not
;; syntax-quote's own `~name`/`~params-or-doc` slots) since `fn`'s
;; multi-arity shape (`(fn ([p1] b1) ([p1 p2] b2))`) has no separate
;; "params" position to unquote into the way its single-arity shape does.
;; Every branch below also runs its params/body through
;; `destructure-fn-body`/`destructure-fn-clauses` (see the section right
;; above) -- a no-op unless a param position is actually a destructuring
;; pattern, in which case it's replaced by a gensym and the body gets
;; wrapped in a reconstructing `let`.

(defmacro defn
  "Defines a function var: `(defn name [params] body...)`, with an
  optional docstring before the params vector: `(defn name \"doc\"
  [params] body...)`. Also supports `fn`'s multi-arity shape:
  `(defn name ([p1] b1) ([p1 p2] b2))`, optionally with a docstring
  first: `(defn name \"doc\" ([p1] b1) ([p1 p2] b2))`. Any param position
  in any arity -- `params` itself or one of its elements -- may be a
  destructuring pattern instead of a plain symbol, exactly like `let`
  (see that section for the pattern grammar); `fn` itself doesn't support
  this."
  [name params-or-doc & more]
  (cond
    (and (string? params-or-doc) (= (type-of (first more)) :vector))
    (let [expanded (destructure-fn-body (first more) (rest more))]
      `(def ~name ~params-or-doc (fn ~(first expanded) ~@(first (rest expanded)))))
    (string? params-or-doc)
    `(def ~name ~params-or-doc ~(cons 'fn (destructure-fn-clauses more)))
    (= (type-of params-or-doc) :vector)
    (let [expanded (destructure-fn-body params-or-doc more)]
      `(def ~name (fn ~(first expanded) ~@(first (rest expanded)))))
    true
    `(def ~name ~(cons 'fn (destructure-fn-clauses (cons params-or-doc more))))))
;; `defn-` needs to attach `^:private` metadata to a symbol that only
;; exists as *data* inside this macro's own expansion (the caller's own
;; `name` argument) -- unlike `^:private sym` reader sugar, which only
;; ever attaches to a symbol actually written in source text, this has no
;; literal symbol in `defn-`'s own source to hang the metadata off. The
;; `with-meta` primitive is the general mechanism for exactly this case:
;; it returns a copy of a symbol with new metadata, callable from
;; ordinary (or macro-time) Logos code rather than only from the reader.

(defmacro defn-
  "Like `defn`, but the resulting var is `^:private` (excluded from
  `require`/`use :refer`). Same optional-docstring, multi-arity, and
  destructuring forms as `defn`."
  [name params-or-doc & more]
  (cond
    (and (string? params-or-doc) (= (type-of (first more)) :vector))
    (let [expanded (destructure-fn-body (first more) (rest more))]
      `(def ~(with-meta name {:private true}) ~params-or-doc
        (fn ~(first expanded) ~@(first (rest expanded)))))
    (string? params-or-doc)
    `(def ~(with-meta name {:private true}) ~params-or-doc
      ~(cons 'fn (destructure-fn-clauses more)))
    (= (type-of params-or-doc) :vector)
    (let [expanded (destructure-fn-body params-or-doc more)]
      `(def ~(with-meta name {:private true}) (fn ~(first expanded) ~@(first (rest expanded)))))
    true
    `(def ~(with-meta name {:private true})
      ~(cons 'fn (destructure-fn-clauses (cons params-or-doc more))))))
;; ---- doc --------------------------------------------------------------------
;; `(doc name)` reads back the `:doc` metadata `def`'s optional docstring
;; argument attaches -- a macro, not a function, so the caller writes the
;; bare name (`(doc defn)`) rather than quoting it themselves; `name`
;; arrives as the unevaluated symbol already, quoted here for `var-doc`
;; (an ordinary function, whose argument otherwise really would be
;; evaluated as a symbol reference).

(defmacro doc
  "`(doc name)` -- returns the docstring `name`'s var was `def`'d with
  (via `defn`/`defmacro`/`defn-`'s optional-docstring form, or `def`'s
  own 3-arg form directly), or `nil` if it has none. `name` is written
  bare, unquoted."
  [name]
  `(var-doc (quote ~name)))
;; ---- ns -----------------------------------------------------------------------
;; `(ns name (:require spec...) (:use spec...))` -- sugar over `in-ns`
;; plus one `require`/`use` call per clause, NOT real file-based namespace
;; loading (Logos has none yet -- `require`/`use` themselves only work
;; against namespaces already loaded into this same `Runtime`; see
;; `Logos.Primitives`'s `load_ns!/2` moduledoc comment). Every spec form
;; is quoted before being spliced into the generated `require`/`use` call
;; so it reaches the primitive as literal data regardless of whether it's
;; a bare namespace symbol (which would otherwise resolve as a variable
;; reference) or a `[ns :as alias :refer [...]]` vector (which is already
;; self-quoting, so this is harmless there too). The three helpers below
;; are private -- safe here for the same reason `build-let` is (they live
;; directly in `logos.core`, see this file's header comment).

(def ^:private ns-quote-specs
  "Wraps every spec form in `(quote ...)` so it reaches `require`/`use`
  as literal data -- see this section's header comment."
  (fn [specs]
    (cond
      (= specs ()) ()
      true (cons (list 'quote (first specs)) (ns-quote-specs (rest specs))))))
(def ^:private ns-clause-form
  "Turns one `(:require spec...)`/`(:use spec...)` clause into the
  corresponding `(require ...)`/`(use ...)` call form."
  (fn [clause]
    (let [kw (first clause)
        specs (ns-quote-specs (rest clause))]
      (cond
        (= kw :require) (cons 'require specs)
        (= kw :use) (cons 'use specs)
        true (throw :invalid-ns-clause clause)))))
(def ^:private ns-clause-forms
  "Maps `ns-clause-form` over every clause `ns` was given."
  (fn [clauses]
    (cond
      (= clauses ()) ()
      true (cons (ns-clause-form (first clauses)) (ns-clause-forms (rest clauses))))))
(defmacro ns
  "`(ns name (:require spec...) (:use spec...))` -- switches to (creating
  if needed) namespace `name`, then runs one `require`/`use` call per
  clause. Sugar over `in-ns`+`require`/`use`, not real file-based
  namespace loading -- see this section's header comment."
  [name & clauses]
  (cons 'do (cons (list 'in-ns (list 'quote name)) (ns-clause-forms clauses))))
;; ---- type predicates ----------------------------------------------------------
;; All six are thin wrappers over the one `type-of` primitive (a single
;; type-tag getter -- see `Logos.Primitives`) rather than one primitive
;; each, so only that one piece of Elixir code needs to know
;; `Logos.Value.t()`'s actual representation; everything else here is
;; ordinary Logos. `string?` is the one exception -- it stays a
;; primitive (see its own comment in `Logos.Primitives`), since
;; `defmacro`'s own bootstrap above needs it before `defn` exists yet to
;; write these as ordinary functions.

(defn nil?
  "True if `x` is Logos's own `nil` value."
  [x]
  (= (type-of x) :nil))
(defn list?
  "True if `x` is a Logos list. `nil` is not a list (though `first`/
  `rest`/`cons` treat it as an empty one) -- see `empty?` in `logos.seq`
  for a predicate that considers both `nil` and `()` empty."
  [x]
  (= (type-of x) :list))
(defn vector?
  "True if `x` is a Logos vector (`[...]`)."
  [x]
  (= (type-of x) :vector))
(defn map?
  "True if `x` is a Logos map (`{...}`)."
  [x]
  (= (type-of x) :map))
(defn set?
  "True if `x` is a Logos set (`#{...}`)."
  [x]
  (= (type-of x) :set))
(defn decimal?
  "True if `x` is a Logos decimal (`10.99M`)."
  [x]
  (= (type-of x) :decimal))
(defn number?
  "True if `x` is an integer, float, ratio, or decimal."
  [x]
  (let [t (type-of x)]
    (or (= t :integer) (= t :float) (= t :ratio) (= t :decimal))))
;; ---- threading macros: ->, ->>, some->, some->>, as->, cond->, cond->> --------
;; Real Clojure semantics throughout. `->`/`->>` need no hygiene at all --
;; each step's expansion mentions the previous step's *form* exactly
;; once, so nothing is ever evaluated twice; `some->`/`some->>` need a
;; single per-step hygienic temp to test-then-reuse a value without
;; re-evaluating it, so (like `and`/`or` above) they lean on syntax-quote's
;; `g#` auto-gensym. `cond->`/`cond->>` need that same per-step temp to
;; appear both *inside* and *outside* one syntax-quote's own text (once as
;; the `let` binding, once inside the conditionally-threaded step built as
;; plain data beforehand) -- `g#` auto-gensym can't do that (it only
;; guarantees consistency *within* one syntax-quote's own literal text),
;; so these two call the `gensym` primitive directly instead and splice
;; the one resulting symbol value everywhere it's needed.

(def ^:private thread-first-step
  "`x` threaded into `form` as the first argument -- a bare symbol `f`
  becomes `(f x)`; a list `(f a b)` becomes `(f x a b)`."
  (fn [x form]
    (cond
      (list? form) (cons (first form) (cons x (rest form)))
      true (list form x))))
(def ^:private thread-last-step
  "`x` threaded into `form` as the last argument -- a bare symbol `f`
  becomes `(f x)`; a list `(f a b)` becomes `(f a b x)`."
  (fn [x form]
    (cond
      (list? form) (concat form (list x))
      true (list form x))))
(def ^:private thread-first
  "Folds `thread-first-step` over `forms`, left to right, starting from
  `x` -- the whole non-short-circuiting expansion `->` needs."
  (fn [x forms]
    (cond
      (= forms ()) x
      true (thread-first (thread-first-step x (first forms)) (rest forms)))))
(def ^:private thread-last
  "`thread-first`'s `->>`-style counterpart."
  (fn [x forms]
    (cond
      (= forms ()) x
      true (thread-last (thread-last-step x (first forms)) (rest forms)))))
(defmacro ->
  "`(-> x form1 form2 ...)` -- threads `x` through each form, inserting
  it as the first argument of each. `x` and every form are each
  evaluated exactly once, in order."
  [x & forms]
  (thread-first x forms))
(defmacro ->>
  "`(->> x form1 form2 ...)` -- like `->`, but inserts `x` as the LAST
  argument of each form instead of the first."
  [x & forms]
  (thread-last x forms))
;; Checks `x` itself for `nil` *before* threading it into the first form
;; -- not just each step's *result* after the fact -- matching real
;; Clojure (`(some-> nil (+ 2))` => `nil`, never attempting `(+ nil 2)`
;; at all). That means the per-step temp has to appear both inside one
;; syntax-quote's own text (the `let` binding, the `nil?` check) and
;; outside it (spliced into the pre-built next step) -- the same
;; cross-scope need `cond->`/`cond->>` have, so these two also reach for
;; the `gensym` primitive directly rather than `g#` auto-gensym.

(defmacro some->
  "`(some-> x form1 form2 ...)` -- like `->`, but short-circuits to
  `nil` (without threading into or evaluating anything further) the
  moment `x` itself, or any step's result, is `nil`."
  [x & forms]
  (cond
    (= forms ()) x
    true
    (let [g (gensym "g")]
      `(let [~g ~x]
        (if (nil? ~g) nil (some-> ~(thread-first-step g (first forms)) ~@(rest forms)))))))
(defmacro some->>
  "`(some->> x form1 form2 ...)` -- `some->`'s `->>`-style counterpart."
  [x & forms]
  (cond
    (= forms ()) x
    true
    (let [g (gensym "g")]
      `(let [~g ~x]
        (if (nil? ~g) nil (some->> ~(thread-last-step g (first forms)) ~@(rest forms)))))))
(def ^:private as->-bindings
  "Builds `as->`'s flattened `let` bindings list: `name` rebound to
  `expr`, then to each of `forms` in turn -- Logos's own `let` already
  binds sequentially, each binding seeing earlier ones (see
  `build-let`'s own comment), which is exactly the rebind-in-place
  `as->` needs."
  (fn [name expr forms]
    (cond
      (= forms ()) (list name expr)
      true (concat (list name expr) (as->-bindings name (first forms) (rest forms))))))
(defmacro as->
  "`(as-> expr name form1 form2 ...)` -- binds `expr` to `name`, then
  evaluates each form in turn with `name` rebound to the previous
  form's result (`expr`, for the first). Unlike `->`/`->>`, `name` may
  appear anywhere in each form -- nothing is spliced in automatically."
  [expr name & forms]
  `(let ~(list->vector (as->-bindings name expr forms)) ~name))
(defmacro cond->
  "`(cond-> x test1 form1 test2 form2 ...)` -- threads `x` through only
  the steps whose paired test is truthy (`->`-style, first-argument
  insertion); an untaken step's value passes through unchanged to the
  next. `x` and each test are evaluated exactly once."
  [x & clauses]
  (cond
    (= clauses ()) x
    true
    (let [test (first clauses)
        form (first (rest clauses))
        more (rest (rest clauses))
        g (gensym "g")]
      `(let [~g ~x]
        (cond-> (if ~test ~(thread-first-step g form) ~g) ~@more)))))
(defmacro cond->>
  "`(cond->> x test1 form1 test2 form2 ...)` -- `cond->`'s `->>`-style
  counterpart."
  [x & clauses]
  (cond
    (= clauses ()) x
    true
    (let [test (first clauses)
        form (first (rest clauses))
        more (rest (rest clauses))
        g (gensym "g")]
      `(let [~g ~x]
        (cond->> (if ~test ~(thread-last-step g form) ~g) ~@more)))))
;; ---- basic numeric/utility functions -------------------------------------------
;; `quot`/`rem`/`apply`/`str` (see `Logos.Primitives`) are the only new
;; Elixir-side additions this whole section needed -- everything below
;; is pure Logos, built from those plus primitives/macros that already
;; existed. `min`/`max`/`comp` call `reduce`/`reverse` (`logos.seq`),
;; which hasn't loaded yet at the point *this* file is being read -- safe
;; regardless, since a `fn` body is only ever resolved against whatever
;; is reachable *when it's actually called*, not when it's defined (see
;; this file's own header comment on bare-symbol resolution), and by the
;; time anyone calls `min`/`max`/`comp`, `logos.seq` has long since been
;; loaded and referred into `logos.core`.

(defn not
  "True if `x` is falsy (`nil` or `false`); false otherwise."
  [x]
  (if x false true))
(defn identity
  "Returns `x` unchanged."
  [x]
  x)
(defn constantly
  "`((constantly v) & any-args)` always returns `v`, ignoring whatever
  arguments it's called with."
  [v]
  (fn [& args] v))
(defn complement
  "Returns a function that returns the boolean opposite of calling
  `pred` with the same arguments."
  [pred]
  (fn [& args] (not (apply pred args))))
(defn inc "`x` plus 1." [x] (+ x 1))
(defn dec "`x` minus 1." [x] (- x 1))
(defn zero? "True if `n` is `0`." [n] (= n 0))
(defn pos? "True if `n` is greater than `0`." [n] (> n 0))
(defn neg? "True if `n` is less than `0`." [n] (< n 0))
(defn even?
  "True if integer `n` is evenly divisible by 2."
  [n]
  (= (rem n 2) 0))
(defn odd?
  "True if integer `n` is not evenly divisible by 2."
  [n]
  (not (even? n)))
(defn mod
  "Floored modulo -- unlike `rem` (whose sign always matches `n`'s), the
  result's sign always matches `d`'s: `(mod -7 2)` => `1` (`(rem -7 2)`
  => `-1`), `(mod 7 -2)` => `-1`. Real Clojure semantics."
  [n d]
  (let [r (rem n d)]
    (if (or (= r 0) (= (< n 0) (< d 0))) r (+ r d))))
(defn min
  "The smallest of `a` and any further arguments, compared via `<`
  (ratio-/decimal-aware)."
  [a & more]
  (reduce (fn [acc x] (if (< x acc) x acc)) a more))
(defn max
  "The largest of `a` and any further arguments, compared via `>`
  (ratio-/decimal-aware)."
  [a & more]
  (reduce (fn [acc x] (if (> x acc) x acc)) a more))
(def ^:private comp-fold
  "Applies each of `fns`, in order, to `value`, threading each result
  into the next -- `comp`'s own fold, once its first (rightmost) function
  has already consumed the caller's actual arguments."
  (fn [value fns]
    (cond
      (= fns ()) value
      true (comp-fold ((first fns) value) (rest fns)))))
(defn comp
  "`(comp f g h)` returns a function that calls `h` with all of its own
  arguments first, then `g`, then `f`, each on the previous result:
  `((comp f g) x)` = `(f (g x))`. `(comp)` returns `identity`."
  [& fns]
  (if (= fns ())
    identity
    (let [rev (reverse fns)]
      (fn [& args] (comp-fold (apply (first rev) args) (rest rev))))))
(defn partial
  "`(partial f a b)` returns a function that calls `f` with `a`/`b`
  prepended to whatever arguments it's later called with."
  [f & preset]
  (fn [& more] (apply f (concat preset more))))
;; ---- case / condp -----------------------------------------------------------------
;; Both pure macros over `cond`/`if`, same spirit as `if` itself. `case`'s
;; own test values are never evaluated (compared as literal data via
;; `=`, quoted in the generated code) -- real Clojure semantics, and the
;; whole reason `case` exists as something other than `cond` sugar:
;; `(case x sym-name 1 ...)` compares `x` against the literal symbol
;; `sym-name`, never resolving it as a variable reference. `condp`'s test
;; values, by contrast, ARE evaluated expressions (its whole point is
;; testing `expr` against each one via an arbitrary predicate, not just
;; `=`). Neither supports `case`'s real-Clojure macro-time-constant
;; *enforcement* (a non-literal test throws at compile time there) --
;; this is pure Logos over `cond`, not a hash-based jump table, so a
;; non-literal test quietly just never matches instead. `condp`'s own
;; `:>>` result-fn form is also out of scope -- real Clojure feature,
;; rarely used, not worth the complexity here.

(def ^:private case-test-forms
  (fn [g tests]
    (cond
      (= tests ()) ()
      true (cons (list '= g (list 'quote (first tests))) (case-test-forms g (rest tests))))))
(def ^:private case-test-form
  "Builds the `=`-comparison form for one `case` test -- `test` may be a
  single literal value, or a list of alternatives any one of which
  matching selects the clause."
  (fn [g test]
    (if (list? test)
      (cons 'or (case-test-forms g (to-list test)))
      (list '= g (list 'quote test)))))
(def ^:private case-cond
  "Builds `case`'s expansion: a chain of `(if (test-form) result ...)`,
  ending in a trailing default form (if given) or a
  `:no-matching-clause` throw."
  (fn [g clauses]
    (cond
      (= clauses ()) (list 'throw :no-matching-clause g)
      (= (rest clauses) ()) (first clauses)
      true
      (list
        'if
        (case-test-form g (first clauses))
        (first (rest clauses))
        (case-cond g (rest (rest clauses)))))))
(defmacro case
  "`(case expr test1 result1 test2 result2 ... default?)` -- evaluates
  `expr` once, compares it via `=` against each `test` in turn (each
  `test` a LITERAL constant, never evaluated; `test` may also be a list
  of alternative literal values, any one matching). Returns the paired
  `result` for the first match, or the trailing unpaired `default` form
  if given; with no default and no match, throws `:no-matching-clause`."
  [expr & clauses]
  ;; `g#` auto-gensym only guarantees consistency *within* one
  ;; syntax-quote's own literal text -- `case-cond`'s own output needs
  ;; the SAME temp both inside this `let` binding and outside it (built
  ;; as plain data), so this calls the `gensym` primitive directly and
  ;; splices the one resulting value everywhere, the same fix
  ;; `cond->`/`cond->>`/`some->` (threading macros, above) already
  ;; needed for the identical reason.
  (let [g (gensym "case")] `(let [~g ~expr] ~(case-cond g clauses))))
(def ^:private condp-cond
  (fn [p g clauses]
    (cond
      (= clauses ()) (list 'throw :no-matching-clause g)
      (= (rest clauses) ()) (first clauses)
      true
      (list 'if (list p (first clauses) g) (first (rest clauses)) (condp-cond p g (rest (rest clauses)))))))
(defmacro condp
  "`(condp pred expr test1 result1 test2 result2 ... default?)` --
  evaluates `expr` once, tries `(pred test expr)` for each `test` in
  turn (each `test` IS an evaluated expression, unlike `case`'s literal
  constants), returning the paired `result` for the first truthy one.
  With no default and no match, throws `:no-matching-clause`."
  [pred expr & clauses]
  (let [p (gensym "pred")
      g (gensym "condp")]
    `(let [~p ~pred ~g ~expr] ~(condp-cond p g clauses))))
;; ---- binding (dynamic vars) -----------------------------------------------------
;; A `^:dynamic` var (e.g. `(def ^:dynamic *out* nil)`) can be
;; thread-locally (per-BEAM-process) rebound for the extent of a
;; `binding` body via `push-thread-binding!`/`pop-thread-binding!`
;; (`Logos.Primitives`) -- the actual per-process override storage and
;; the read-side hook (`Logos.Eval`'s `get_var_value_dynamic_aware/3`)
;; are necessarily Elixir-side (BEAM process-dictionary access isn't
;; reachable from pure Logos), but `binding` itself is an ordinary macro
;; over the existing `try`/`finally` special form -- no new special form
;; needed, matching how `if`/`case`/`condp` are all built directly on
;; `cond`. `binding-pushes`/`binding-pops` walk `bindings`'s flat `[name
;; value name value ...]` list two at a time, the same style
;; `build-let`'s own pairs-walking uses.

(def ^:private binding-pushes
  (fn [flat]
    (cond
      (= flat ()) ()
      true
      (cons
        (list 'push-thread-binding! (list 'quote (first flat)) (first (rest flat)))
        (binding-pushes (rest (rest flat)))))))
(def ^:private binding-pops
  (fn [flat]
    (cond
      (= flat ()) ()
      true
      (cons
        (list 'pop-thread-binding! (list 'quote (first flat)))
        (binding-pops (rest (rest flat)))))))
(defmacro binding
  "`(binding [*a* 1 *b* 2] body...)` -- thread-locally (per-BEAM-process)
  rebinds each `^:dynamic` var to its paired value for the extent of
  `body`, restoring the previous value once `binding` returns -- even if
  `body` throws, via `try`/`finally` under the hood. Only vars `def`'d
  with `^:dynamic` meta may be bound this way; pushing onto an ordinary
  var throws `:not_dynamic`. Nested `binding` forms shadow correctly
  (innermost wins, unwinding to the next-outer value on exit) since the
  per-var override is a stack, not a single slot. Bindings do **not**
  propagate into a `spawn`/`spawn-link`/`spawn-monitor`ed child process
  -- a fresh process starts with no active bindings of its own, matching
  real Clojure (a bare new thread doesn't inherit dynamic bindings
  either, only `bound-fn` does). Rebinding the innermost active binding
  from within its own `binding` scope (Clojure's `set!`) is not
  supported."
  [bindings & body]
  (let [flat (to-list bindings)]
    `(do
      ~@(binding-pushes flat)
      (try
        (do ~@body)
        (finally ~@(binding-pops flat))))))
